From a29039825f3ec49dcc09d9342b36fd4dcb4f4359 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nieyin345 <nieyin0703@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:57:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(save): a host-installed save guard at the four document
 save call sites

atomic-write.ts already draws the line: "This primitive protects temporary-file
ownership. The caller/Host must still enforce the source version and file lease
for concurrent editors." This is that missing half.

guardedDocumentSave({ target, format, write }) is the single exit for saving:
with no guard installed it calls ctx.write() and is byte-for-byte the previous
behaviour (standalone GenOffice). With Mcode's guard installed it compares the
open-time revision with the on-disk one inside the same file lease and refuses
without writing a byte when they differ.

write is a callback, not something the guard does itself: comparison and write
must happen in one critical section, or two saves both read the old revision,
both pass, and the second silently overwrites the first.

Seams are at the four save call sites, not at atomicWriteFile:
  html/markdown  writeTextAtomic
  pdf            savePdfToPath (atomicWriteFile also serves merge/insert/sign)
  slides         slides:save, via stagedAtomicWrite

stagedAtomicWrite is new here because slides had no atomic write at all:
savePptxToFile pipes straight into createWriteStream on the target. Its failure
path follows atomic-write.ts exactly -- delete staging only when the staging
write itself failed; if the rename fails keep the completed staging, fall back
to an in-place write, and only then clean up. Deleting the staging in a finally
would destroy the only complete copy precisely when the save reports failure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
